Thus helicity separates into:

	•	Tw_i: internal material twist;

	•	Wr_i: centerline coiling or writhe;

	•	Lk_{ij}: mutual linking/braiding;

	•	\Gamma_i: circulation carried by each tube.

﻿

For parallel straight tubes,

﻿

Tw_i=Wr_i=Lk_{ij}=0,

﻿

so their relative helicity is zero even if the vacuum supports background circulation.

﻿

Then:

	•	a single helical tube has self-helicity;

	•	a Q=3 braid has mutual-helicity/linking contributions;

	•	higher braid nesting adds relational helicity at larger scales.

﻿

That maps directly onto the superhelix/braid distinction.

﻿

Revised particle-formation mechanism

﻿

The corrected sequence is

﻿

\text{straight BEC tube}

\rightarrow

\text{twist injection}

\rightarrow

\text{twist-to-writhe bifurcation}

\rightarrow

\text{localized helical excitation}

\rightarrow

\text{metastable particle}.

﻿

A particle is therefore not the existence of a worldtube. It is a non-vacuum state of one or more already-existing worldtubes.

﻿

A useful state classification is:

﻿

State	Geometry

Vacuum	Straight, aligned, zero relative helicity

Wave/radiation	Transient curvature or twist without stable closure

Single particle	Localized, persistent self-helicity/holonomy

Hadron	Three-tube Interbraid configuration

Larger composite	Braid nesting among already-formed excitations

﻿

What rotation now does

﻿

A rotational component of expansion injects twist or vorticity into the vacuum congruence:

﻿

\nabla v=D+W,

\qquad

W\neq0.

﻿

Differential expansion supplies axial strain; W supplies rotation. When the straight state becomes unstable, internal twist converts into centerline writhe and produces an H(s)H coil.

﻿

But uniform rotation of every tube would merely produce a rotating or helically biased vacuum. Particle localization requires some nonuniformity:

	•	a phase defect;

	•	a local instability;

	•	a boundary interaction;

	•	a tube collision;

	•	a closure/holonomy condition;

	•	a reconnection event.

﻿

Mathematically, particle formation occurs when the vacuum solution loses stability:

﻿

\delta^2E[\gamma^{(0)}]

\quad\text{develops a zero or negative mode}.

﻿

That is precisely a bifurcation problem.

﻿

The Planck-mass issue changes too

﻿

If every vacuum tube possesses a Planckian/Kerr-like core scale, those cores are not separate additive Planck-mass particles. They belong to the vacuum baseline.

﻿

Observed particle energy would instead be the excess energy

﻿

E_{\rm particle}

﻿

E[\text{helical/tangled configuration}]

﻿

E[\text{straight vacuum configuration}].

﻿

This offers a structurally sensible route around the earlier “every electron contains a Planck mass” problem. It does not solve it numerically—the subtraction and gravitational readout must still be derived—but it places the Planck-scale contribution in the substrate rather than adding it repeatedly to every excitation.

﻿

So the revised core picture is:

﻿

Spacetime vacuum is a multiplicity of straight BEC worldtubes. Helicity distinguishes excited geometry from vacuum. A particle is a localized, metastable helical or braided excitation of those tubes.

